    {
      "id": "comment-cdc070cb-dac4-4de4-a617-39f94ebfdd4b",
      "type": "COMMENT",
      "name": "Welcome",
      "position": [
        50,
        50
      ],
      "params": {
        "text": "# Quantum Basics - Beyond Classical Bits\n\nWelcome to quantum computing! Here we explore qubits.\n\n## To Do and Notice\n\n1. **Superposition**: A qubit can be 0 AND 1 at the same time\n2. **Measurement**: Observing \"collapses\" the state\n3. **Probability**: Each state has an amplitude that determines likelihood\n\n## Classical vs Quantum\n\n- Classical bit: 0 OR 1\n- Qubit: a|0> + b|1> (superposition)\n\nThe magic happens when we use this to compute in parallel!\n\n**Challenge**: What happens when you measure multiple times?",
        "width": 450,
        "show_on_start": true
      },
      "inputs": {},
      "outputs": {},
      "weights": {},
      "collapsed": false,
      "color": "#5c4a3d",
      "tags": [],
      "description": "Tutorial introduction"
    }
